    "case_id": "190",
    "case_summary": "A 52-year-old Japanese woman presented with a 2-year history of swelling of her right upper eyelid, which had discharged pus for the previous 10 months without any trauma to her head. She was otherwise healthy. She denied any symptoms, including fever, headache, or nasal discharge.\n\nPhysical examination revealed an immovable scarlike node, 11 × 7 mm, on her right upper eyelid discharging pus.\n\n- Imaging Studies:    1. MRI findings:       - Title: T2-weighted and contrast-enhanced MRI       - Description: High signal on T2-weighted image and ring enhancement in the right frontal sinus without any abnormal lesions in the orbital or intracranial tissue        2. CT findings:       - Title: Axial and coronal CT sections       - Description: Opacification on right frontal sinus, right ethmoid sinus, and right maxillary sinus and expansion of the right frontal sinus and right ethmoid sinus without any bone destruction        - Histopathology:       - Title: Sinus mucosa examination       - Description: Mild inflammatory infiltrate with fibrosis, lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate, few eosinophils and no sign of malignancy",
